      <a href="/tools/gps-accuracy/" class="btn btn-primary">Open Accuracy Monitor →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Enable Wi-Fi & Bluetooth Scanning (Assisted GPS / A-GPS)</h2>
  <p>
    A pervasive misconception is that disabling Wi-Fi saves battery without hurting location precision. In reality, mobile operating systems rely heavily on <strong>Assisted GPS (A-GPS)</strong> and <strong>Wi-Fi Positioning Systems (WPS)</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Faster Time-to-First-Fix (TTFF):</strong> Downloading the satellite orbital almanac directly from orbit via 50 bps radio signals takes between 30 seconds to 12 minutes in a "cold start". With Wi-Fi/cellular enabled, your phone downloads current satellite ephemeris over the internet in under <strong>1.5 seconds</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>BSSID Ambient Fingerprinting:</strong> Google and Apple maintain massive global databases of known Wi-Fi router MAC addresses (BSSIDs). Even without connecting to a router, passively detecting nearby Wi-Fi beacons narrows your position to <strong>10–25 meters</strong> indoors where satellite signals cannot penetrate.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Enable "Precise Location" in Browser & OS Permissions</h2>
  <p>
    Modern mobile operating systems include privacy toggles that limit web browsers to "Approximate Location" (a generalized radius of 1 to 5 kilometers) rather than exact satellite coordinates. If your location appears in the wrong neighborhood, this permission is almost always the cause.
  </p>

  <div class="step-container">
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">iOS</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Apple iPhone & iPad (iOS 16 / 17 / 18)</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Open <strong>Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; Location Services</strong>. Select <strong>Safari Websites</strong> (or Chrome). Ensure <strong>Allow Location Access</strong> is set to <em>"While Using the App"</em> and toggle <strong>Precise Location</strong> to <strong>ON</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">AND</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Android Devices (Samsung, Pixel, Xiaomi)</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Open <strong>Settings &gt; Location &gt; App permissions</strong>. Tap <strong>Chrome</strong> (or your browser). Select <em>"Allow only while using the app"</em> and ensure the <strong>"Use precise location"</strong> switch is turned <strong>ON</strong>. Additionally, enable <strong>Google Location Accuracy</strong> under Location Services.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">MAC</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">macOS (Safari & Chrome)</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Navigate to <strong>System Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; Location Services</strong>. Ensure Location Services is checked for your browser. In Chrome, click the site settings tune icon next to the URL bar and set Location to <em>"Allow"</em>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">WIN</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">Windows 10 / 11 PC</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Go to <strong>Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; security &gt; Location</strong>. Turn <strong>Location services</strong> ON and toggle <em>"Let desktop apps access your location"</em> to ON. Desktop computers without built-in GPS cards rely on Wi-Fi router triangulation and IP routing (learn more on our <a href="/my-location/ip/">IP Location page</a>).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Calibrate the Magnetometer (Device Compass)</h2>
  <p>
    When your location indicator in mapping apps displays an erratic heading beam or spins unpredictably, the internal magnetometer has suffered magnetic drift. Metallic phone cases, magnetic wallet attachments, and car dashboard mounts are common culprits.
  </p>

  <h3>Figure-8 Recalibration Technique:</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Open your default mapping application (Google Maps or Apple Maps) or our <a href="/tools/latitude-longitude/">Latitude & Longitude Map</a>.</li>
    <li>Hold your phone firmly with your arm extended.</li>
    <li>Smoothly sweep the device through the air in a large <strong>figure-8 motion</strong> three to five times.</li>
    <li>Watch the blue location cone: it will instantly narrow from a broad arc into a focused directional beam.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>4. Mitigate Multipath Reflection & Urban Canyons</h2>
  <p>
    In high-density downtown areas surrounded by glass and steel skyscrapers ("urban canyons"), satellite signals bounce off buildings before reaching your phone's internal antenna:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <strong>Multipath Delay:</strong> Because GPS calculates distance using speed-of-light travel time, a reflected signal that travels an extra 150 meters before hitting your receiver registers as an artificial 150-meter position displacement.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <strong>How to fix:</strong> Move toward an open street intersection, pedestrian plaza, or park where your receiver has an unobstructed view of the upper atmosphere. Avoid positioning yourself directly under metal awnings, dense tree canopies, or concrete overpasses.
  </p>

